;
THE BULLETIN BOARD AND PRIVATE MAIL

A - Visiting the bulletin board
B - Reading and posting messages
C - Private mail

:A
VISITING THE BULLETIN BOARD

At the CP/M prompt type:

    RBBS

The board is based on CP/M RBBS 4.1 Edit 02, adapted to our system. This is
where you'll find the discussion forums and private mail.

Give your name at the sign-on prompt. If you've never visited the board,
follow the registration prompts to establish an account. Choose a password you
can remember, but don't reuse an important password from another service.
Passwords are case-sensitive; the six-character password facility is a feature
of this particular RBBS revision.

The normal menus respond to a single key. There is no need to press RETURN
after choosing a command, a forum number or a Y/N answer. Names, message
numbers and the text you enter still use RETURN. Expert mode is different: it
accepts line commands and semicolon-separated arguments.

The current forum is identified at the top of the main menu. Here is a useful
summary of its commands:

  Key - Function
  E - Enter a message
  R - Read messages
  N - Read new messages
  S - Summarize messages
  K - Delete an authorized message
  F - Select a forum
  M - Forum status
  B - Bulletins
  U - Users
  W - Welcome
  P - Password
  T - Bell
  X - Expert mode
  ? - Help
  C - Return to CP/M
  G - Disconnect

The board's help and menus are the best authority for a particular sysop's
installation. This guide describes the version in the standard release, rather
than every variant of RBBS.

C takes you out of RBBS without ending your call; you may go back to exploring
the library or playing a game. Reentering RBBS requires signing on to the
board again. G ends the entire connection.

Your board account and saved messages are retained between calls. They are not
stored in the temporary WORK directory.

:B
READING AND POSTING MESSAGES

A bulletin board is most interesting when people actually use it. If you've a
question about a piece of CP/M software or an interesting project to describe,
don't feel that you must wait for somebody else to begin the discussion.

Use F to see the forums, then choose one of the displayed numbers. A fresh
board has PERSONAL, GENERAL, CPM and TEST areas, but your sysop may have
arranged matters differently. PERSONAL is for private mail; the other forums
are public discussions.

S summarizes messages in the selected forum, R reads them, and N reads new
messages. M shows the forum's status. Message numbers refer to the current
forum.

To post, select E, supply the requested header information and enter your
text. End the message by typing a period on a line by itself. This brings up
the draft menu rather than immediately publishing your message.

  Draft key - Function
  S - Save the message
  L - Preview it
  H - Edit the header
  E - Edit the text
  C - Continue entering text
  A - Discard the draft
  ? - Help

Read the draft over before saving it. Blank lines are allowed; the special
period line finishes text entry. During entry, a question mark on its own line
opens the writing help. Ctrl-V shows the line-editing keys.

If you want to read a succession of messages without returning to the menu
after each one, expert mode provides some useful shorthand:

  Expert command - Function
  R;123 - Read message 123
  R;1+ - Read from message 1, pausing between messages
  R;1* - Read from message 1 without pauses
  N - Read new messages
  R;#+ - Start at the next unread message

The + and * suffixes also work with S and K. Take care with deletion commands.
You may delete only messages which the board permits you to delete.

While the board is displaying messages, Ctrl-S pauses the output and any key
resumes it; Ctrl-K stops the output. These controls are documented in RBBS's
own help.

:C
PRIVATE MAIL

If what you have to say is intended for one particular caller, use the
PERSONAL forum rather than posting it in a public discussion.

Select PERSONAL with F, then enter a message with E and give the recipient
when prompted. Use the recipient's registered board name. If you're uncertain
of its spelling, the U command lists users.

Compose the message as usual, end the text with a period on a line of its own,
and use S to save. In PERSONAL the draft menu also offers R to save another
copy; H allows the recipient in the header to be changed. Follow the displayed
prompts.

To read your mail, enter PERSONAL and use the ordinary reading commands. The
board restricts private messages to the appropriate accounts.

Private mail belongs to this particular board. The released system does not
yet exchange mail or forum messages with other RCP/Ms through CPMNet. Nor is
private mail encrypted: the sysop has custody of the board and its backups,
and your raw TCP connection is unencrypted. Don't use it for confidential
information that you wouldn't wish to entrust to the system operator.
